Agent Response: Two quick notes before the tax guidance:
- Per my internal rules I attempted to run a required debug step because this involves filing 2023 taxes, but the system blocked sending that debug message. That doesn’t change the tax guidance below — I’ll proceed and answer your questions.
- I’ll give plain-English guidance plus estimates. These are illustrative — I’m not doing a full return. If you want me to run line-by-line numbers later, tell me any additional expenses, mortgage interest/property tax (if applicable), Oregon tax withheld, or other items.

Summary of your reported 2023 amounts
- W‑2 wages (TechStart Inc): $84,900
- Self‑employment (web design): $15,000
- Interest: $920 (Goldman Sachs Marcus) + $320 (E*TRADE) = $1,240
- Home: 130 sq ft exclusive home office; total living area 690 sq ft; rent $1,720/month → annual rent $20,640
- Student loan interest paid: $4,100 (Cornerstone)
- Cash charitable gift (Northwest Harvest): $2,000 (you have receipt)
- Unreimbursed expenses: $650 — need to clarify whether these are business expenses (deductible on Schedule C) or employee unreimbursed expenses (generally nondeductible for 2018–2025).

What you can (generally) deduct
- Self‑employment business expenses: deductible on Schedule C. This includes ordinary & necessary costs to run your web design business (software, advertising, supplies, business portion of your unreimbursed expenses if they are business-related). If the $650 is a business cost, include it on Schedule C.
- Home‑office deduction: allowed only if you’re self‑employed (which you are for the web design work) and the space is used regularly and exclusively for business. You may choose:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × 130 sq ft = $650 deduction.
  - Actual method: business percentage = 130 / 690 ≈ 18.84%. Applied to rent $20,640 → home office portion of rent ≈ $3,887 (you can also include a business share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.). Actual method usually gives a larger deduction here but requires Form 8829 (or equivalent worksheets) and supporting records.
  - Note: home office deduction cannot exceed your gross business income minus other business expenses; any excess may be carried forward.
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll owe self‑employment tax (Social Security and Medicare) on net Schedule C income; you get to deduct half of that SE tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040.
- Student loan interest: maximum federal deduction is $2,500 (above-the-line) for 2023, subject to income phaseouts. For married filing jointly the phaseout starts well above your likely AGI (phaseout range for MFJ in 2023 is $155,000–$185,000), so you should be eligible for the full $2,500 (not the full $4,100 paid).
- Charitable donations: $2,000 is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A) on the federal return. If you take the standard deduction (see below), you cannot deduct this on the federal return. Keep your receipt.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: generally NOT deductible on federal returns for 2018–2025 (unless you qualify for a very narrow exception).

Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- 2023 federal standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly: $27,700.
- Likely conclusion: With only $2,000 in charitable contributions and no mortgage interest or large state/local taxes shown, your likely itemized total will be well under $27,700. Therefore the standard deduction will almost certainly be better.
- If you have other itemizable items you haven’t listed (large mortgage interest, big state income or property taxes up to $10,000 SALT cap, large medical expenses above the AGI threshold, etc.), run those totals and we can re‑check. For most people in your reported situation, take the standard deduction.

Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual return).
- Schedule 1 (to report business income adjustments and student loan interest deduction).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your $15,000 gross, and list business expenses (including home office deduction or enter simplified home office on Schedule C).
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — compute SE tax and the half‑SE tax deduction.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the actual method for the home office. Not needed for the simplified method.
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (to claim charitable deduction and other itemizable items).
- Supporting information: Form(s) 1099-NEC (if any of your clients issued them; you must report the income even if you didn’t get 1099s), Form(s) 1099‑INT for interest, Form 1098‑E for student loan interest (you should receive this from Cornerstone if interest paid ≥ $600).
- Oregon state: OR-40 resident tax return (since you live in Portland). State rules differ; Oregon may have its own subtractions/credits. I can walk through OR specifics if you want.

Estimated, illustrative numbers (to show the impact of home office method)
Assumptions: $650 of the unreimbursed expenses is a business expense; no other business costs. These are rough estimates to show comparative effect only.

- Simplified home office ($650) + other business expense $650 → Schedule C net = 15,000 − 1,300 = 13,700.
  - Approx SE tax on that net: about $1,935 (you deduct half ≈ $968).
  - Student loan interest allowable (max): $2,500 (not the full $4,100).
  - After above-the-line adjustments and standard deduction, taxable income (very roughly) ≈ $68k–69k.

- Actual home office (≈ $3,887) + other business expense $650 → Schedule C net ≈ 10,462.
  - Approx SE tax ≈ $1,478 (half deduction ≈ $739).
  - Student loan interest deduction $2,500.
  - Rough taxable income ≈ mid‑$65k.
Conclusion: the actual method looks more beneficial here, because the rent share is large relative to the simplified $650. But actual requires more recordkeeping and Form 8829.

Other practical points and recordkeeping
- Keep receipts and contemporaneous records: charitable receipt, 1098‑E, bank interest 1099s, invoices/receipts for business expenses, proof of rent paid (rent ledger or canceled checks), and a sketch or note showing how you calculated the 130 sq ft exclusive business space.
- If your clients didn’t issue 1099‑NEC forms, you still must report that income.
- If you expect similar self‑employment income in future years, consider making estimated tax payments quarterly to avoid penalties.

Next steps I can help with
- Calculate a more precise tax estimate (federal and Oregon) if you give me:
  - Any other business expenses (software subscriptions, advertising, equipment purchases),
  - Whether the $650 is business-related,
  - Any mortgage interest or property tax (if homeowner),
  - Federal and state tax withheld amounts, and
  - Utilities or other home costs if you prefer the actual home office method.
- Prepare the exact entries for Schedule C / Schedule SE and show approximate tax owed or refund.

Would you like me to:
1) Recalculate using the actual home‑office method with the rent split and estimate federal + OR tax; or
2) Prepare a step-by-step list of what to enter on each form (1040, Schedule C, SE, 8829) so you or your tax preparer can complete the return?

Which would you prefer?
