Agent Response: Thanks — useful detail. I’ll cover (1) what’s likely deductible, (2) whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, (3) how to treat the home office (actual vs simplified), (4) self-employment & SE tax issues, and (5) the federal & Oregon forms and documents you’ll need. A few quick clarifying questions are at the end so I can give exact numbers.

1) Quick summary / likely outcome
- Your rough total gross income for 2023: $84,900 (W‑2) + $15,000 (web design) + $920 + $320 = $101,140.
- Student loan interest: only up to $2,500 of interest is deductible on your federal return (subject to MAGI limits). If your MAGI is similar to your gross (~$100k) for MFJ you will be under the typical phaseout threshold and should be eligible for the full $2,500, but I’ll confirm if you want me to compute MAGI precisely.
- Charitable gift ($2,000) is deductible only if you itemize.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 (TCJA) unless they were self-employed business expenses. If that $650 is a business expense for the web design work, you can deduct it on Schedule C.
- Likely outcome: unless you have substantial other itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, large state/local or property taxes, large medical expenses), the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly (2023) — $27,700 — will be larger than itemizing with just a $2,000 charity. So you will most likely take the standard deduction federally.

2) Self-employment income and taxes
- Report web‑design income on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). Even if you didn’t receive 1099s, you report the $15,000.
- Deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (the $650 qualifies if it’s business-related).
- You’ll owe self‑employment (SE) tax on net self‑employment earnings (Schedule SE). SE tax is ~15.3% on ~92.35% of net earnings; you also get to deduct one-half of SE tax as an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).
- If you didn’t make quarterly estimated tax payments and expect tax due > $1,000, you may owe estimated tax penalties — consider paying/filing accordingly.

3) Home office — how to handle and quick calculations
You said the office is 130 sq ft exclusively and the home is 690 sq ft. That is about 130/690 = 18.84% of the home.

Two ways to claim:
A) Simplified method
- $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 130 * $5 = $650 deductible on Schedule C.
- No Form 8829; easy recordkeeping. Good when actual allocable expenses are low.

B) Regular (actual) method
- Deduct your business percentage of allowable home expenses. For a renter, that generally includes a prorated share of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.
- Annual rent: $1,720 × 12 = $20,640. Your home-office share of rent ≈ 20,640 × 0.1884 ≈ $3,887.
- If you have utilities, internet, renter’s insurance, include the business portion of those as well — you may get substantially more than $650 using actual method.
- For the actual method you normally complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) or report the allowable portion on Schedule C; the simplified method does not use Form 8829.

Which method to pick?
- Compare the simplified $650 to the actual-share amount (rent share + share of utilities, etc.). Use the one that gives the higher deduction. If your only home-office expense is rent and no separate utilities, the actual method (≈ $3,887) is likely better.

Important: Home office deductions are only allowed against self‑employment income. You cannot use the home office deduction to reduce W‑2 income.

4) Student loan interest
- You can deduct up to $2,500 of qualified student loan interest on Form 1040 (Schedule 1) if you meet the income/eligibility rules.
- You’ll need Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer showing interest paid in 2023. If you actually paid $4,100 in 2023, you can only deduct up to $2,500 (subject to phaseouts). If $4,100 is total historical interest (not paid in 2023) we need to confirm the amount paid in 2023.

5) Charitable contribution ($2,000)
- Deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A. Keep the receipt/proof of donation.
- No federal “above‑the‑line” deduction for cash gifts to charities in 2023 (unless special one-off rules applied in earlier pandemic years).

6) Unreimbursed expenses ($650)
- If those are business expenses for your web design work (materials, software, supplies), deduct on Schedule C.
- If those are unreimbursed employee expenses (for a W‑2 job), they are not deductible for 2018–2025.

7) Forms you’ll need (Federal)
- Form 1040 (individual return).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for the $15,000 web design income and business expenses (including home office).
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax.
- Schedule 1 — for adjustments (student loan interest deduction and half of SE tax is included as an adjustment).
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (only if you use the actual method for the home office). If you use the simplified method, you do NOT use Form 8829.
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (for the $2,000 charitable gift and any other itemizable items).
- Supporting: 1099-NEC/1099-MISC (if you received them), 1099‑INT (from Marcus / E*TRADE), Form 1098‑E (student loan interest), W‑2 from TechStart, records/receipts for business expenses and charitable contributions.

Forms/documents for Oregon (state)
- Oregon resident return: Form OR‑40 (or OR‑40‑N for certain nonresidents — but as a resident use OR‑40).
- Oregon generally conforms to federal treatment for many items, but check whether itemizing on Oregon gives benefit vs Oregon standard deduction. Keep the same documentation: W‑2, 1099s, 1098‑E, receipts.
- Oregon may require different schedules for adjustments — I can give a state-specific checklist if you want.

8) Records to gather / checklist
- W‑2 from TechStart.
- All 1099s (NEC for clients, 1099‑INT from banks/brokers, 1099‑B if you had sales).
- Form 1098‑E (student loan interest).
- Rent records (lease and rent payment evidence) and utility bills if using actual home office method.
- Receipts for charitable donations (Northwest Harvest receipt).
- Receipts/invoices for business expenses (the $650 and any other supplies, software, subscriptions).
- Mileage logs if you used a vehicle for business.
- Prior-year tax return for reference.

9) Next steps and clarifying questions
I can produce exact numbers and a sample calculation of taxable income and estimated federal tax if you want — but I need a few clarifications:
- Is the $4,100 the amount of student loan interest you paid in 2023 (and do you have a 1098‑E for 2023)?
- Is the $650 unreimbursed expense a business expense for your web design work (i.e., can it be reported on Schedule C), or is it unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job?
- Are you a renter (I assumed yes because you listed monthly rent) and do your rent payments cover utilities, or are utilities billed separately?
- Did you receive any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC for the $15,000 in web design income? If not, is $15,000 the gross receipts you received in 2023?

If you want, I can:
